'''Mercadero''' ({{IPA-bl|meɾkaˈdeɾo}}; {{Literal translation|marketplace}}), colloquially known as ''Merca'', is a major {{W|business district}} and {{W|pedestrian zone}} located in the [[Ampurias]] borough of [[Castejón]]. Mercadero is {{W|Latin America}}'s largest and densest business district, covering {{convert|5956|ha}}, with 142 glass and steel buildings (of which 29 are completed {{W|skyscraper}}s), 360,000 daily workers, and {{convert|3500000|m2}} of office space. Around its [[Arco Merca]] and [[W:Esplanade|esplanade]] ("la Portapí"), Mercadero contains many of both [[List of tallest buildings in Castejón|Castejón]]'s and [[List of tallest buildings in Balisca|Balisca's]] tallest high-rises. [[Galleria Merca]], a large shopping mall in Mercadero dating back to 1875, has over 1,000 stores, 125 restaurants and two 12-screen movie theatres.

Despite historically being the main central business district of Castejón, the development of secondary, tertiary and quaternary business districts in [[Trinacria]]'s [[Basilio]], [[Expansión]]'s [[Sant Dídac]] and [[Charxes|Charxes']] [[Gistaín]] has led to a relocation of companies out of Mercadero and subsequent {{W|urban decay}}. This issue was further accelerated by the {{W|COVID-19 pandemic}} and the shift to {{W|work from home|remote work}}, reflecting the steep declines in office occupancy rates across Balisca at the time. As such, Mercadero is of particular interest to the [[Casajuan Plan]].

The district forms the southernmost extremity of the ''[[Aixo Sol]]'' of [[Castejón]], which radiates to the northwest and southeast from the [[Arco Merca]]. It is trisected by the [[Tistaina Canal]]. 

